'create\'\n      ORDER BY parent_film_id, comp_film_id, date);\n    \n\n"""\n\nCase 5:\n"""### Question: What is the best way to get a derived status column based on existing result\n\nI have a table :\n\n    \n    \n    ------------------------\n    testId |    runId   |result \n    ------------------------\n    **1    | 11         |0**\n    **1    | 12         |1**\n    **1    | 13         |1**\n    **1    | 14         |1**\n    **2    | 21         |0**\n    **3    | 31         |1**\n    **4    | 41         |1**\n    **4    | 42         |1**\n    **4    | 43         |1**\n    **5    | 51         |0**\n    **5    | 52         |0**\n    **5    | 53         |0**\n    **6    | 61         |1**\n    **6    | 62         |0**\n    **6    | 63         |1**\n    \n\n* * *\n\nFor a test there can be multiple run/execution. Each run have a result. here\nfor result column, 0 is fail and 1 is pass. I want to query \\--if all the run\nPASS for test, the OverallStatus is PASS \\--If all the run Faile for a test,\nthe OverallStatus is FAIL \\--If some of them pass and some of them fialed then\nOverallStaus is DEFECT\n\n## I want to get an output from the above table like\n\n## testId |numOfRun |OverallStatus\n\n1 | 4 |Defect\n\n2 | 1 |FAIL\n\n3 | 1 |PASS\n\n4 | 3 |PASS\n\n5 | 3 |FAIL\n\n6 | 3 |Defect\n\n* * *\n\n* * *\n\n\n\n### Answer:\n\nYou can use conditional aggregation\n\n    \n    \n    select testId,\n           numOfRun,\n    case when numOfRun = pass then \'pass\'\n        when numOfRun = fail then \'fail\'\n        else \'defect\'\n        end as OverallStatus \n    from (\n        select testId,\n        count(*) numOfRun,\n        sum(case when result = 0 then 1 else 0 end) as fail,\n        sum(case when result = 1 then 1 else 0 end) as pass\n        from table\n        group by testId \n    ) t\n    \n\n"""'}], 'response': "The query plan shows that the query is using a combination of bitmap heap scans, bitmap index scans, and unique scans. The query is also using external merge sort to sort the results.\n\nTo improve the performance of this query, you could consider the following:\n\n1. Add an index on the `event_type` column to speed up the bitmap index scans.\n2. Consider rewriting the query to use a single `BitmapOr` operation instead of two separate ones.\n3. If possible, try to reduce the number of rows being scanned by adding more conditions to the `WHERE` clause or using a more efficient join.\n4. Consider using a different sorting method if the external merge sort is taking too long.\n\nAs for your second question, you can use conditional aggregation to get the derived status column based on the existing result. Here's an example:\n\n```sql\nSELECT testId,\n       COUNT(*) AS numOfRun,\n       CASE\n           WHEN SUM(CASE WHEN result = 0 THEN 1 ELSE 0 END) = 0 AND SUM(CASE WHEN result = 1 THEN 1 ELSE 0 END) > 0 THEN 'PASS'\n           WHEN SUM(CASE WHEN result = 0 THEN 1 ELSE 0 END) > 0 AND SUM(CASE WHEN result = 1 THEN 1 ELSE 0 END) = 0 THEN 'FAIL'\n           WHEN SUM(CASE WHEN result = 0 THEN 1 ELSE 0 END) > 0 AND SUM(CASE WHEN result = 1 THEN 1 ELSE 0 END) > 0 THEN 'DEFECT'\n           ELSE 'PASS'\n       END AS OverallStatus\nFROM table\nGROUP BY testId;\n```\n\nThis query uses conditional aggregation to count the number of passes and failures for each test, and then uses a `CASE` statement to determine the overall status based on those counts.", 'time': 3.782801389694214}
